# Common blackbird

The common blackbird is the familiar thrush of European gardens and woodland,
the male all black with a yellow bill and the female a warm brown. Its rich,
fluting song is delivered from a high perch from late winter onward, and the
alarm rattle at dusk is one of the most familiar sounds of a suburban garden. It
feeds on earthworms and windfall fruit taken from the ground, running a few
paces and pausing to listen. The female builds a cup nest of grass wedged into a
hedge and supported from below, and raises two or three broods in a season. See
[[turdidae]].
